    "agent_last_message": "I've written the tool as a single file, `output/main.js`, using only the Node.js standard library. In a few manual runs it matched the spec on lines, case-insensitive counting, punctuation kept as part of words, tie-breaking and an empty file. Those runs are the only testing so far.\n\nHow to run it:\n```\nnode output/main.js --input file.txt --output result.json\n```\n\n- **Counting:** characters are counted as bytes, as the spec says. A line is counted for each newline, plus one more if the file doesn't end with a newline. Words are split on the six whitespace characters the spec lists.\n- **Top words:** the 10 most frequent, sorted by count from highest to lowest, with ties in alphabetical order.\n- **Exit codes:** you get 1 for a missing, unknown or duplicate flag, a flag with no value, or an input file that is missing, unreadable or not a regular file. Anything unexpected gives 2.\n\nDecisions you may want to change:\n- **\"Malformed\" input:** the spec doesn't define it. I treat a file that isn't valid UTF-8 as malformed, so it exits with 1. If you'd rather analyze any file regardless of encoding, that check is easy to remove.\n- **Flag formats:** both `--input path` and `--input=path` work.\n- **Uppercase outside basic English letters:** I lowercase all Unicode letters, so accented and other non-English capitals are folded too.\n- **Alphabetical ties:** these use a plain character-code comparison, not the system's language-aware sort order.",
